  "query_preview": "(Today through Thursday)...\nIssued at 247 AM EDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nEarly This Morning...\n\nThunderstorms from late evening Thursday sent another outflow \nboundary into the far southern forecast area. So far early this \nmorning, only isolated to widely scattered showers and thunderstorms \nhave developed in the wake of this boundary. \n\nGiven the latest trends in radar and in short term guidance, will \nkeep PoPs no higher than the chance category. As the main surface \nboundary continues to sink south, the highest PoPs will gradually \nshift south. CAPE has been diminished, so not expecting any severe \nstorms. Gusty winds will remain possible, especially south where \nwhat CAPE is left is highest.\n\nAdvected Layered Precipitable Water data from satellite shows \nmoisture being brought into the area from the Gulf as usual but also \nfrom both the southwestern USA in the monsoon areas. Values near 2 \ninches are across the area currently. Thus, the threat for locally \nheavy rain continues. With this threat, am hesitant to cancel the \nFlood Watch, even though training of heavy rain looks less likely.\n\nToday and Tonight...\n\nThe main surface boundary will continue to sink south across the \narea. Little upper support will be around to work with the boundary. \nThus odds of convection are low. Will only have some patchy slight \nchance PoPs around in the south for most of the day, but can't rule \nout an isolated shower elsewhere. The Flood Watch will likely be \ncanceled early.\n\nA good temperature gradient will exist today, with highs in the 70s \nnortheast to around 90 in the far southwest. Heat indices in the far \nsouth will be near 100 again today.\n\nMuch of tonight looks dry with forcing remaining away from the area.\n\nSaturday into Tuesday...\n\nAn unsettled pattern will continue for much of this period as \nvarious upper and surface waves move through, causing the front to \nmeander north and south across central Indiana. Moisture still looks \nto be plentiful, so locally heavy rain will remain a threat for all \nor parts of the area through Monday.\n\nOn Saturday, there looks to be a low end severe threat where \nisolated severe storms could produce damaging winds. \n\nHighs this weekend will vary from around 80 far north to around 90 \nfar south. \n\nCooler air will work in for Monday and Tuesday with highs falling to \naround 70 to the lower 70s by Tuesday.\n\nWednesday and beyond...\n\nSurface high pressure will nudge into the area mid to late week next \nweek and may keep this period dry. Will have to watch for an \ninverted trough just to the east, which could bring a few showers if \nit ends up being farther west than expected. Temperatures will be \nmuch closer to normal with highs in the 70s and lows in the 50s.",
